-- title:
--   Noetherian local domain with principal quotient is factorial
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, Noetherian, and local (so it has a unique maximal ideal $\mathfrak m =$ `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A`), and let $t \in A$ be an element such that the quotient $A/(t)$ by the ideal generated by $t$ is again an integral domain and is a principal ideal ring, i.e. every ideal of $A/(t)$ is generated by one element. Note that the assumption that $A/(t)$ is a domain forces $(t)$ to be a prime ideal of $A$, in particular a proper one, so $t \in \mathfrak m$; the element $t$ is allowed to be $0$, in which case the hypothesis says that $A$ itself is a principal ideal domain. The conclusion is that $A$ is a unique factorisation monoid: in Mathlib's formulation, $A$ satisfies the ascending divisibility-chain condition and every irreducible element of $A$ is prime, equivalently $A$ is a unique factorisation domain.
--
--   This is the local-algebra criterion that factoriality descends from a principal quotient $A/tA$ (a field or a discrete valuation ring when $A$ is Noetherian local), proved through Kaplansky's characterisation of unique factorisation domains by the existence of prime elements in nonzero prime ideals. It serves as the source of normality and factoriality statements used further on, being cited by the companion result that such a local ring is integrally closed, by the factoriality of regular local rings of Krull dimension at most two, and by the statement on primes lying under the maximal ideal at an unramified point with Dedekind quotient.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsLocalRing_uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isPrincipalIdealRing_quotient.lean

